WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:04.950
in the 1960s in the United States with the
passing of the Civil Rights Act.

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:11.370
We saw what many consider to be sort of the
official end of the absolute most explicit

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.250
and formal types of government racism.

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:21.669
When we ended mandated segregation, we outlawed
many other types of racial discrimination.

00:00:21.669 --> 00:00:26.230
But since then, and really to this day, black
and Hispanic Americans continue to suffer

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:33.200
from a variety of social and economic inequities
with regard to wealth, education, health outcomes,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:35.829
cycles of crime and incarceration.

00:00:35.829 --> 00:00:37.859
The list is a mile long.

00:00:37.859 --> 00:00:45.070
When we're talking about socioeconomic adversities
that disproportionately impact populations

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.480
of color over the past 50 or 60 years.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.559
In the time since the civil rights movement,
we've seen many racial equality movements

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:57.570
try to illuminate and put a spotlight on and
address these problems.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:04.019
And these movements tend to acknowledge that
these problems are the ripple effects of historical

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:07.350
institutional racism, like slavery and Jim
Crow.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.210
And these movements have pushed for solutions
that are designed to help these groups in

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:18.210
particular as remedies for problems that impact
them disproportionately.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:25.120
This has taken the form of affirmative action
scholarships for minority students, bussing

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:31.110
to integrate schools, federal financial support
for historically black colleges, government

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.840
programs that help minority businesses.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:40.500
These are all forms of action that solve or
try to solve race based problems in the United

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.070
States.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:47.740
Over the past half century, what we've also
seen consistently in parallel is pushback

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.440
against these racial equality initiatives.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:56.590
Here are a few clips of some popular conservatives
making comments which I believe accurately

00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:05.760
sum up, sum up the prevailing right wing narrative
on systemic racism with regard to socioeconomics.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:10.009
One could certainly say during the Jim Crow
era when when, you know, African-Americans

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:12.700
couldn't vote, that systemic racism.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.470
You have to look things in the system that
are contributing to racism.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.940
Is systemic racism still a reality today?

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:21.490
It is not a real reality.

00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:23.560
It is the opposite of reality.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.120
It's all an illusion.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.830
You can point to me, to the law or the practice
that's in place that makes that somehow keeps

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:30.830
me out.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:34.990
If there's two people that are working the
exact same and putting out the scene as early

00:02:34.990 --> 00:02:38.760
as a month after, you're going to tell me
that as a black person, I can't get ahead.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:39.760
It doesn't exist.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.920
There are lots of rich black people in America,
and I know lots of poor white people in America.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.650
So what is it?

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:50.190
Because black people in the United States
are disproportionately poorer than white people

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:51.430
in the United States?

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:54.400
It's actually yeah, it's ignoring the real
problem, which is how do we get poor people

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:56.069
to be richer?

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:57.800
Systemic racism does not exist.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.740
There are no laws in America.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.510
Meaning the system doesn't mean racism doesn't
exist.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:03.510
Racism exists.

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:04.690
There are people who are racist.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:08.190
And you should do everything you can to show
them that their ideas are wrong and that you

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:11.280
should not be judging someone based on their
race, of course.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:15.590
But the idea that there is systemic racism,
meaning that there are laws in place that

00:03:15.590 --> 00:03:19.310
treat black people differently or Asian people
differently or white people differently simply

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.020
doesn't exist.

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.550
Treat you if you can get an economy that is
as free as possible, you will have more and

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:29.800
more people, regardless of the color of their
skin, being able to partake in an economy.

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:38.020
So those comments cover a bunch of the predominant
views from the right on systemic racism.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:43.500
Those were clips of three of the most popular
right wing online broadcasters.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:48.810
So the first thing I want to point out is
they are defining systemic racism so narrowly

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:54.330
that it would only refer to racism by the
government and only the most explicit and

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:58.950
official forms of government racism like Jim
Crow, for example.

00:03:58.950 --> 00:04:04.099
And that's how they're able to say that systemic
racism doesn't exist.

00:04:04.099 --> 00:04:09.360
If someone's constantly asserting something
that everyone already accepts is true, such

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:15.440
as slavery and Jim Crow no longer exist, it's
likely that they're either accidentally or

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:20.759
purposefully misunderstanding what they are
trying to argue against.

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:21.859
That's what's happening here.

00:04:21.859 --> 00:04:27.800
I've never encountered someone who asserts
that systemic racism is a problem today while

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:30.509
defining racism in such a narrow way.

00:04:30.509 --> 00:04:35.610
People usually use the term systemic racism
to denote any type of racial biases, formal

00:04:35.610 --> 00:04:41.719
or informal biased system, or someone acting
on behalf of a system which could take the

00:04:41.719 --> 00:04:47.879
form of racial bias by police officers or
by a law enforcement system.

00:04:47.879 --> 00:04:51.229
Racial bias by people hiring at a company.

00:04:51.229 --> 00:04:56.759
Racial bias in education like a standardized
test that puts certain racial groups at a

00:04:56.759 --> 00:04:57.759
disadvantage.

00:04:57.759 --> 00:05:01.370
It doesn't even necessarily have to be racism
by an individual.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:07.169
Importantly, it could just be a system doing
something that in practice disadvantages members

00:05:07.169 --> 00:05:09.999
of a certain group on the basis of their race.

00:05:09.999 --> 00:05:17.000
And I think that the concept requiring a little
bit of nuance is exactly why some people get

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:18.000
confused.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.620
Some people get defensive about the word racism
being involved.

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:27.009
So maybe we can have a separate conversation
about finding a better term, which is fine.

00:05:27.009 --> 00:05:33.270
But that's what people mean when they say
there is active systemic racism present in

00:05:33.270 --> 00:05:35.009
the year 2020.

00:05:35.009 --> 00:05:39.419
So I think a lot of conservatives are confused
about the definition.

00:05:39.419 --> 00:05:44.560
If they act as though it's synonymous only
with slavery and Jim Crow or some of them

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.229
are straw manning in bad faith to avoid actually
engaging with the reality on the ground.

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:55.520
But the conservatives I played in the clips,
what they also seem to be saying is that systemic

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.069
racism as someone like me would define it
doesn't exist.

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:04.409
Their sentiment is slavery and Jim Crow are
over.

00:06:04.409 --> 00:06:07.960
Black people no longer face any types of disadvantages.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.319
There are no systemic biases against black
people.

00:06:10.319 --> 00:06:15.330
There are no lingering effects from historical
institutional inequality, unfairness that

00:06:15.330 --> 00:06:20.849
still leave the deck stacked against minority
individuals.

00:06:20.849 --> 00:06:28.129
Black and white people on average have identical
opportunity to succeed, they would assert.

00:06:28.129 --> 00:06:33.719
The other thing they seem to be saying is
that because they don't believe those racial

00:06:33.719 --> 00:06:40.819
biases exist, we shouldn't be looking at minority
poverty through the lens of race in order

00:06:40.819 --> 00:06:45.659
to solve solve it with racial social justice
action.

00:06:45.659 --> 00:06:51.849
They say we should instead look at black poverty,
for example, as an arbitrary piece of poverty

00:06:51.849 --> 00:06:53.349
as a whole.

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:58.539
And we should try to solve black poverty merely
by fixing poverty as a whole.

00:06:58.539 --> 00:07:04.029
And in addition to saying that racial solutions
to, for example, black or Hispanic poverty

00:07:04.029 --> 00:07:05.539
are unnecessary.

00:07:05.539 --> 00:07:11.800
Often they will add that those racial solutions
dismiss, ignore or be little white poverty,

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:19.589
and they discount the personal responsibility
of nonwhite individuals to improve their.

00:07:19.589 --> 00:07:25.029
Own lives, but it's not surprising that that's
the direction conservatives would go, because

00:07:25.029 --> 00:07:30.370
each of those pieces fits beautifully into
the conservative media narratives that we've

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:31.370
been following.

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:35.410
For example, racial inequality is generally
overblown.

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:37.599
Identity politics is bad.

00:07:37.599 --> 00:07:39.889
Government intervention is the wrong way to
fix anything.

00:07:39.889 --> 00:07:44.960
Capitalism and meritocracy ultimately are
the answers to everything.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.759
Will conservatives like these are not realizing
is that there are social and economic problems

00:07:50.759 --> 00:07:56.249
today in the United States that affect minorities
in specific ways.

00:07:56.249 --> 00:08:01.869
Black folks as a group do have certain types
of particular disadvantages.

00:08:01.869 --> 00:08:07.560
And to admit that is a reality doesn't mean
that you're deriding the poverty of other

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.189
races or in general.

00:08:09.189 --> 00:08:12.899
And it doesn't mean you're devaluing anybody's
individual responsibility.

00:08:12.899 --> 00:08:17.279
It doesn't mean that there aren't things we
can also be doing generally to help everybody

00:08:17.279 --> 00:08:19.860
in a situation of hardship, regardless of
race.

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:22.509
Of course, we want to be thinking about those
solutions, too.

00:08:22.509 --> 00:08:24.649
And I talk about those solutions every day.

00:08:24.649 --> 00:08:30.179
I might even agree with somebody like Ben
Shapiro when he says that the mere existence

00:08:30.179 --> 00:08:36.750
of a black white poverty gap alone is not
necessarily a justification for using race

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.899
as the context to fix black poverty.

00:08:39.899 --> 00:08:46.540
The fact that black folks are impoverished
at a higher rate than white people by itself

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:52.050
might not justify racial solutions as opposed
to solutions that improve the lives of all

00:08:52.050 --> 00:08:53.050
groups.

00:08:53.050 --> 00:08:57.759
But what these people on the right seem never
to recognize is that certain opportunity gaps

00:08:57.759 --> 00:08:59.639
also still exist.

00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:05.339
Not only do black folks in many respects have
worse outcomes, on average, black folks also

00:09:05.339 --> 00:09:11.410
have diminished opportunity to improve those
outcomes compared to white people on average,

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:12.759
on average.

00:09:12.759 --> 00:09:14.629
That's what these conservatives are missing.

00:09:14.629 --> 00:09:19.120
And it's the reason why it is actually useful
to explore certain solutions that are tailored

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:21.950
towards certain particular racial inequalities.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:25.449
In some ways, black and white poverty can
be different.

00:09:25.449 --> 00:09:30.540
There are many metrics that illustrate gaps
between a black person and a white person

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.580
of the same income.

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:38.000
There was a groundbreaking study by Stanford
and Harvard researchers from 2018, which combines

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:43.850
census data on race with IRS tax returns,
allowing them to compare the income of parents

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:49.800
with the income of their grown children over
the span of 1989 to 2015 for the purpose of

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:54.270
tracking intergenerational mobility, which
is the degree to which children economically

00:09:54.270 --> 00:09:56.339
surpass their parents or fall behind.

00:09:56.339 --> 00:10:02.550
And what they found is that downward mobility
occurs at a much higher rate among black Americans

00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:04.300
than among white Americans.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:07.480
When you compare black and white people with
similar incomes.

00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:11.430
This was true even among the children of higher
income parents of both races.

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:16.529
Children of white parents in the top fifth
of the income distribution have a 41 percent

00:10:16.529 --> 00:10:19.480
chance of staying at that income level.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:25.040
Children of black parents in that highest
income level only have an 18 percent chance

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:26.440
of staying at that income level.

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:30.769
So regardless of how much money your parents
make, if you are black, you are less likely

00:10:30.769 --> 00:10:35.009
to achieve the economic status of your parents
or higher.

00:10:35.009 --> 00:10:40.139
Sadly, I can already imagine some people are
going to butt in with, oh, well, maybe it

00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:44.270
has something to do with intelligence or single
parenthood or something like that.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:51.220
The study found that the black white mobility
gap is entirely driven by black and white

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:52.250
males.

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:58.050
There was very little gap between black and
white females, which would rule out genetic

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:01.139
factors between races as an explanation.

00:11:01.139 --> 00:11:06.670
If that's your sort of thing to look at, the
study also looked at family structure and

00:11:06.670 --> 00:11:12.000
it found that the huge gap is there, regardless
of whether children came from one or two parent

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:19.069
homes, which debunks the conservative idea
that lower marriage rates are responsible.

00:11:19.069 --> 00:11:25.320
But a factor that does tend to powerfully
predict economic mobility is the average income

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:28.579
of the neighborhood in which a child was raised.

00:11:28.579 --> 00:11:34.110
A study by Harvard economists published in
2015 showed overwhelming evidence for this.

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:39.459
The researchers looked at 16 years of tax
records to track pairs of children and parents,

00:11:39.459 --> 00:11:44.430
and they found that the affluence of a child's
neighborhood is the single best predictor

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:46.759
of their long term upward mobility.

00:11:46.759 --> 00:11:52.170
The amount of money in an area where a child
grows up is strongly correlated with that

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:57.190
child's ability to achieve more socioeconomic
success than their parents.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:12:03.029
A study was published in 2015 by the American
Academy of Political and Social Science, conducted

00:12:03.029 --> 00:12:05.399
by researchers at Stanford.

00:12:05.399 --> 00:12:10.950
By looking at census data which found that
among black and white families with similar

00:12:10.950 --> 00:12:17.120
incomes, white families are much more likely
to live in neighborhoods with higher average

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:18.120
incomes.

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:19.430
And of course, this means.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:25.069
That the white family is more likely to have
access to better schools, better childcare,

00:12:25.069 --> 00:12:28.150
better parks, better transportation options.

00:12:28.150 --> 00:12:33.459
The list of advantages in a high income neighborhood
relative to a low income neighborhood is a

00:12:33.459 --> 00:12:34.829
very, very long list.

00:12:34.829 --> 00:12:41.029
And between a black and white family of similar
income, the difference between the average

00:12:41.029 --> 00:12:43.770
incomes of their neighborhoods is not small.

00:12:43.770 --> 00:12:45.069
It's huge.

00:12:45.069 --> 00:12:49.500
So to put all of this together, we have the
mobility gap between black and white people

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:50.970
of similar incomes.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:56.080
The mobility of anyone, regardless of race,
tends to be predicted by the average income

00:12:56.080 --> 00:12:58.270
of the neighborhood where they grew up.

00:12:58.270 --> 00:13:03.029
And we see that a black family with the same
income as a white family is more likely to

00:13:03.029 --> 00:13:05.550
live in a poor neighborhood.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:10.810
And that's because black people tend to live
near other black people and black people have

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.600
lower incomes than white people on average.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:19.200
And of course, that is what we call racial
housing segregation, the geographic separation

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.200
of races.

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:21.600
And it is a racial issue.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:29.279
So we've demonstrated one of many examples
of a social problem in 2020, which can be

00:13:29.279 --> 00:13:31.300
usefully analyzed in terms of race.

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:33.640
And it warrants racial justice action.

00:13:33.640 --> 00:13:38.290
The problem is that black folks have less
upward mobility than white folks.

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:42.089
The cause of the problem is racial housing
segregation.

00:13:42.089 --> 00:13:46.950
And the solution would be integrating housing
to a greater degree.

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:51.639
There are so many other examples of social
problems today that demand racial justice

00:13:51.639 --> 00:13:54.910
solutions in education, health care, the justice
system.

00:13:54.910 --> 00:14:00.310
I'd have nowhere near enough time in this
video to go into each and every one of them.

00:14:00.310 --> 00:14:02.220
So let's flesh out this example.

00:14:02.220 --> 00:14:04.329
We're dealing with housing segregation.

00:14:04.329 --> 00:14:08.649
Before we start talking about solutions to
segregation, we should contemplate how the

00:14:08.649 --> 00:14:11.720
housing segregation that exists today came
to be.

00:14:11.720 --> 00:14:18.110
A lot of people think that people of the same
races ended up living in the same areas accidentally

00:14:18.110 --> 00:14:20.240
or as a result of personal choice.

00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:25.470
But actually, most of today's racial segregation
in every city across America results from

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:28.370
explicitly racist government policies.

00:14:28.370 --> 00:14:34.399
Throughout the late 19th and early 20th century,
many American cities passed zoning ordinances

00:14:34.399 --> 00:14:39.319
prohibiting black people from moving onto
a block that was majority white.

00:14:39.319 --> 00:14:44.629
Starting in 1934, the federal government gave
white people better access to affordable mortgages

00:14:44.629 --> 00:14:50.930
while designating black neighborhoods in cities
across America as zones where banks should

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:52.370
not offer mortgages.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:57.569
This was known as redlining, which encouraged
white people to move to homogenously white

00:14:57.569 --> 00:14:58.569
suburbs.

00:14:58.569 --> 00:15:01.199
And it trapped black people in decaying neighborhoods.

00:15:01.199 --> 00:15:07.130
Also, in the 1930s, the Public Works Administration
built tens of thousands of public housing

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:12.199
complexes all over the country, all of which
had a designation for which racial group was

00:15:12.199 --> 00:15:13.579
allowed to live there.

00:15:13.579 --> 00:15:15.740
And almost all of them were either all white
or all black.

00:15:15.740 --> 00:15:20.899
And much of the racial segregation that it
caused is still present today.

00:15:20.899 --> 00:15:26.199
There are plenty of other instances of government
mandating racial housing segregation.

00:15:26.199 --> 00:15:32.329
Deliberate, codified government racism is
what gave us almost all of the housing segregation

00:15:32.329 --> 00:15:33.329
we see today.

00:15:33.329 --> 00:15:38.290
And now white people and black people are
more segregated from one another than white

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:44.790
people are segregated from Asian or Hispanic
people as examples and more so than poor and

00:15:44.790 --> 00:15:47.579
nonpoor, people are segregated from one another.

00:15:47.579 --> 00:15:52.870
And black white segregation puts a hugely
unfair burden on black people.

00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:57.579
It has much worse effects for a black family
living in a black neighborhood who have the

00:15:57.579 --> 00:16:01.470
same income and education as a white family
living in a white neighborhood.

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:06.680
The average middle class black family lives
in a neighborhood with a higher poverty rate

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:10.809
than the average low income white person.

00:16:10.809 --> 00:16:15.660
Segregation also prevents home values from
appreciating in black neighborhoods because

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:19.439
of white folks reluctance to buy a home in
a black neighborhood.

00:16:19.439 --> 00:16:24.689
Even when all other attributes of homes and
neighborhoods are equal, which, by the way,

00:16:24.689 --> 00:16:29.980
is an example of systemic racism happening
actively in the year 2020, and it's a type

00:16:29.980 --> 00:16:33.269
of systemic racism with a huge impact on black
wealth.

00:16:33.269 --> 00:16:37.449
Since someone's home is often their largest
financial asset.

00:16:37.449 --> 00:16:43.060
So the crucial thing here is that higher poverty
rates in the neighborhood means lower quality

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:46.320
resources for everyone who lives there.

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:50.839
Segregation is the biggest barrier between
black people in higher income wealth.

00:16:50.839 --> 00:16:54.499
Better education, health care, employment
opportunities and so on.

00:16:54.499 --> 00:16:58.630
One might even be able to say that today the
government's refusal to integrate housing

00:16:58.630 --> 00:17:03.600
more effectively to fix the problems that
government created originally could itself

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:07.210
be another example of ongoing systemic racism
in 2020.

00:17:07.210 --> 00:17:12.510
The government not doing something is just
as much a policy decision as the government

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:13.839
doing something.

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:18.960
Sure, someone like Ben Shapiro can sit back
and say, yeah, okay, whatever happened in

00:17:18.960 --> 00:17:19.960
the.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:21.530
Passed with racism and segregation was bad.

00:17:21.530 --> 00:17:26.660
But now that the government has stopped actively
mandating segregation, we can just wipe the

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:27.660
slate clean.

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:30.930
We can just blame individual black people
for being poor.

00:17:30.930 --> 00:17:34.880
But that would be a complete misunderstanding
of the forces that created these problems

00:17:34.880 --> 00:17:38.320
and the forces that would be necessary to
fix them.

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:43.790
Government has a ton of power, obviously,
and it used this power to segregate for a

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:45.340
long time.

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:49.440
That same amount of power would be required
to undo it.

00:17:49.440 --> 00:17:55.990
This is where we get to the distinction between
desegregation and integration.

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:59.690
Desegregation is when a government stops separating
people.

00:17:59.690 --> 00:18:03.960
Integration is the process of un separating
those same people, which has not happened.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:09.420
So we're currently stuck in a state of segregation,
essentially in a way.

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:13.720
The Civil Rights Act did not accomplish as
much as conservatives seem to think it did.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:16.670
It didn't hit the reset button on racial inequality.

00:18:16.670 --> 00:18:20.840
Racial groups in the United States are not
on a level playing field.

00:18:20.840 --> 00:18:26.060
So what can government do to counteract segregation
as a way to give black people more economic

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:27.060
opportunity?

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:31.240
Well, to start with, we could reinstitute
the Obama era policy of requiring localities

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:36.610
that receive federal money for housing to
identify and try to remedy housing practices

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:38.440
that promote racial bias.

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:42.440
The policy that was suspended by the Trump
administration, which Donald Trump completely

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:45.340
blew out of proportion in terms of what it
did.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:50.510
And Donald Trump has made clear that he has
no interest in dealing with that issue and

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:56.240
in fact, in his personal past has a history
of racial housing discrimination.

00:18:56.240 --> 00:19:01.630
Commenting on his elimination of this anti-racist
policy, Trump actually tweeted, I am happy

00:19:01.630 --> 00:19:06.430
to inform all of the people living in their
suburban lifestyle dream that you will no

00:19:06.430 --> 00:19:12.340
longer be bothered or financially hurt by
having low income housing built in your neighborhood.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:13.340
Enjoy.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:16.400
That's another example of institutional racism
in 2020.

00:19:16.400 --> 00:19:18.190
If I've ever seen one.

00:19:18.190 --> 00:19:24.420
Another step we could take to counter segregation
by reducing housing discrimination is to increase

00:19:24.420 --> 00:19:30.340
the use of fair housing testers who are individuals
who pose as potential buyers and renters to

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:37.150
gather information on possible instances of
illegal racial housing discrimination because

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:40.960
this discrimination tends to push black people
out of white areas.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:45.330
Tests like these have been funded by the Department
of Housing and Urban Development, and they've

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.810
uncovered a staggering amount of racial discrimination.

00:19:48.810 --> 00:19:54.910
For example, they did a test in Chicago in
2018 and identified racial discrimination

00:19:54.910 --> 00:19:58.300
at twenty seven percent of the time.

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:02.360
There's some more current systemic racism
as an example for you.

00:20:02.360 --> 00:20:08.060
Nevertheless, HUD does not fund these tests
anywhere near the level that matches how pervasive

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:10.970
and destructive we observe this problem to
be.

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:15.530
We have to be better about enforcing anti
housing discrimination laws that are already

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.000
on the books.

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:21.520
Government could also be bolstering inclusionary
zoning policies, which require homebuilders

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:26.060
to make sure that portions of new housing
units are affordable to low and moderate income

00:20:26.060 --> 00:20:30.500
people to eliminate the financial barriers
that often end up keeping poor black families

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:33.570
from finding an affordable home in a good
neighborhood.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:38.880
We could also be using more federal housing
subsidies to help lower income black families

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:40.450
move into better neighborhoods.

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:45.910
The low income housing tax credit could be
used to develop subsidized rental housing

00:20:45.910 --> 00:20:48.220
in nonpoor neighborhoods.

00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:53.300
There is great new research that illustrates
that what we have been doing with this has

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:57.870
had huge payoffs, especially for the kids
of low income families.

00:20:57.870 --> 00:21:06.050
There are many other strategies we could be
using to disperse black families so that lower

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:08.280
income households aren't so concentrated.

00:21:08.280 --> 00:21:12.890
New policies that can be implemented and through
better funding and enforcement of initiatives

00:21:12.890 --> 00:21:16.540
that already exist could be significantly
beneficial.

00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:22.970
But I wanted to explore just racial housing
segregation as an example to illustrate some

00:21:22.970 --> 00:21:26.340
points about systemic racism and how much
can be done about it.

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:31.760
It's a topic that is always relevant and worthy
of a deep dove, but especially right now with

00:21:31.760 --> 00:21:35.680
everything going on with Black Lives Matter,
the discussions around race that are happening

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:40.420
over the past few months while they've had
the country's attention, black folks in the

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:47.810
U.S. and allies of all kinds have been trying
to shed light on a number of really important

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:50.240
problems in the country.

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:54.200
Things that this country has to start dealing
with, it's way overdue.

00:21:54.200 --> 00:21:58.850
But I'm seeing some people actually confused
about what are even the starting points for

00:21:58.850 --> 00:22:00.580
these conversations.

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:03.450
The reality is racism can take many different
forms.

00:22:03.450 --> 00:22:07.820
Some people just have an overly simplistic
conception of what racism is.

00:22:07.820 --> 00:22:10.380
People start talking about systemic racism.

00:22:10.380 --> 00:22:13.000
Conservatives respond with I have no idea
what you're talking about.

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:14.020
We have no more slavery.

00:22:14.020 --> 00:22:15.200
Jim Crow is over.

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:16.880
We ended that a long time ago.

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:19.700
So racial injustice no longer exists.

00:22:19.700 --> 00:22:21.960
That perspective misunderstands.

00:22:21.960 --> 00:22:24.410
Deliberately or accidentally.

00:22:24.410 --> 00:22:25.950
What systemic racism is.

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:32.900
And it ignores the fact that systemic racism
exists and is just another form of racism.

00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:40.900
We also have to refute the common conservative
notion that black hardships can only be productively

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:45.280
discussed in the context of hardship or poverty
in general.

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:49.580
Instead of acknowledging additional racial
components that are often involved, there

00:22:49.580 --> 00:22:55.600
are some issues that are more accurately understood
when you take into account racial injustices

00:22:55.600 --> 00:22:56.600
that may be at play.

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:56.610
So that you can solve them with specially
catered solutions.

